-- Associated module sheaves for Serre, 2026-10-29; independent opening audit.
-- Includes all 26 owned modules: native restrictions, scalar compatibility,
-- overlap clearing/killing, elementary and iterated localisation comparison;
-- relative-principal-cover separation and gluing; actual associated module
-- system, basic-open evaluation, arbitrary finite-cover separation/sheafhood;
-- plus generic module sheaves, kernels, products, structure/restriction,
-- linear inverses, compatible-family sections, and ring localisation bridge.
-- The relative gluing proof uses the formal Nullstellensatz to express g^p
-- in the powered covering ideal, then divides the corrected numerator by g^p.
-- The arbitrary-open sheaf proof refines finitely and glues each basic
-- component uniquely; its chosen-gluing construction is opaque after Fits.
-- Structural carriers remain transparent for dependent module families.
-- Reviewed openings are the stated operations, characterising projections,
-- lattice and module carriers, and existing finite-family/subtype definitions.
-- The map restriction accessor also compares the native localisation module
-- with its restriction of scalars: its by-definition check uses the named
-- additive-law projections, carrier set and fraction/image definitions.
-- No function-extensionality or truncation-elimination construction is
-- reopened by downstream clients. Every listed audit must explicitly
-- report nothing else opened; checking the exit status alone is insufficient.
-- The native algebra kernel and sheaf kernel still duplicate the elementary
-- subtype-module construction; consolidating that is future Serre-only cleanup.
-- Quasi-coherence, compact/general section localisation and affine module
-- reconstruction remain separate obligations; they are not asserted here.
-- This batch adds actual associated functoriality and identity/composition,
-- native localisation-chart squares, scalar-compatible basis equivalences,
-- their naturality and the global associated restriction equivalence.
-- Global extension and equivalence use named finite-cover generic theorems.
